{
  "requirement": "After one valid judgment, the harness MUST write the subject text, submitted\ndetermination and rationale, and recorded inputs using the existing evidence\nschema. {cite}`requirements.ci.schema`\nBy default, it MUST write the subject's evidence file. With `--stdout`, it MUST\nemit a single evidence JSON object followed by a newline to stdout and MUST NOT\ncreate or change evidence files or directories. All three outcomes MUST be\nwritten to the selected destination as completed assessments; their validation\nstatus MUST follow {cite}`requirements.ci.determination`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the persistence requirement is met.** The code marked {impl} covers every obligation, and the tests cover the main outcomes and failure cases. I found no unmarked implementing code. I did not find any behavior in the {impl} code that another definition fails to specify.\n\n## Implementation\n\n- **Record contents.** `record` (`src/authoring/mod.rs` ~788) builds the evidence object after a valid judgment.\n  - For requirements and examples it writes the subject text under the `requirement` or `example` key, then `determination`, `rationale`, `references` (the reference-set digest) and `inputs`. This is the existing schema in `requirements.ci.schema`.\n  - `assess` calls `record` only after `converse` returns an accepted judgment, so nothing is written for an invalid one.\n  - `Judgment::to_value` (`src/judgment.rs`) serializes all three outcomes: `true`, `false` and `{\"FIXME\":[...]}`.\n- **Default destination.** `configured_assessment` (`src/authoring/mod.rs` ~378) handles `EvidenceOutput::File` by creating the parent directory and writing `AuthoringSubject::evidence_path`. That path is `evidence/<name>.json`, or the citation-review path for citation subjects.\n- **Batch destination.** `write_record` (`src/authoring/batch.rs`) writes to the same path through a temporary file and an atomic persist. `PendingAssessment::persisted` then marks the evidence as written.\n- **`--stdout`.** `EvidenceOutput::Stdout` writes the pretty-printed JSON plus a trailing newline to stdout and flushes. It runs no `create_dir_all` or `fs::write` for evidence. Usage logs go to the separate state location, so no evidence file or directory is touched.\n- **All three outcomes.** The write path does not branch on the outcome. True, false and FIXME are all written, and `usage.record.evidence_written` is set afterward. Validation status comes from `requirements.ci.determination`, which has its own tests.\n- **Marking.** The writers and the path function carry {impl} markers. `PendingAssessment` itself carries only a {cite}, but it is a plain data holder, and the methods that act on it (`persisted`, `write_record`) carry {impl}. I do not count that as a gap.\n\n## Tests\n\n- **`stdout_emits_only_evidence_json_without_changing_project_files`** (`tests/authoring_cli.rs`) runs true, false and FIXME with `--stdout`. It checks:\n  - stdout parses as a single JSON object ending in a newline, with the right `requirement`, `determination`, `rationale` and `inputs`;\n  - the existing evidence file is byte-identical afterward;\n  - when the evidence directory did not exist, it is still absent;\n  - verbose and non-verbose runs are both covered.\n- **Default file mode.**\n  - `ollama_cli_delivers_sources_records_fixme_and_keeps_usage_content_free` writes a FIXME record and checks that validation accepts it. It then flips the record to false and checks that validation rejects it.\n  - `false_is_a_completed_assessment` and `negative_assessment_records_additional_reads_and_fails_validation` write false records and check that validation reports them. `false_is_a_completed_assessment` also checks that the usage outcome is `completed`.\n  - `verbose_single_author_reports_only_false_rationale_without_source_inputs` checks the written file's determination and rationale.\n  - The batch tests in `tests/authoring_batch.rs` write true and false records, and validation then passes. They also check that `references` equals the digest.\n- **Failure cases.**\n  - `failed_evidence_write_keeps_the_accepted_judgment_in_usage` makes the write fail and checks the failed outcome and `evidence_written=false`.\n  - The denied-read tests check that no evidence is written for the aborted assessment while the other assessments' evidence is preserved.\n  - `explicit_untracked_reads_abort_without_another_request_or_evidence_write` checks that the existing evidence is left unchanged.\n- The live Bedrock test checks `--stdout` creates no evidence directory, and it is skipped when `CI` is set.\n\n## Minor observations (not gaps)\n\n- Single-subject file mode uses a plain `fs::write`, while batch mode replaces the file atomically. The requirement does not specify atomicity.\n- The stdout tests do not directly assert the `references` field. The batch tests and the validation round-trips cover it.",
  "references": "c51ee997790716abb90d24ddd3e340402f10480b9cc3cac01f4dc7eed2034142",
